
  No accounts, no server, no event log, no subscriptions table — "tasks you care about" is just "your tasks," and
  "change" is just "the due date arrived." That's four of the five layers deleted. Good outcome.

  [1mNow the constraint that shapes everything, and it's a nasty one:[0m a static web page cannot reliably wake itself up to
  tell you something.

  - While the tab is [1mopen[0m: trivial. A timer and a banner. Zero infrastructure.
  - Browser running, tab [1mbackgrounded or closed[0m: needs the Notifications API plus a service worker — and service workers
    can't reliably schedule a future wake-up. The APIs designed for this (Notification Triggers, Periodic Background
    Sync) are Chrome-only, install-gated, heuristic, or never shipped. You'd get reminders [3msometimes[0m.
  - [1mEverything closed[0m, notification on your phone: genuinely requires either a server pushing via Web Push, or a
    native/installed app with OS-level local notifications. This reintroduces the backend we just deleted — or changes
    the platform.

  This is the fork, and it's expensive to change later: it decides whether we stay a static page or acquire a server.
  Options one and two are cheap and reversible. Option three is a different project.

  My recommendation is [1mtab-open reminders plus browser notifications when the tab is merely backgrounded[0m — honest about
  its limits, buildable in days, and it tells you whether you'd even use this before you pay for push infrastructure.
  Most people discover that a task app they check daily doesn't need OS-level push at all.
